<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serabit el-Khadim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Serabit el-Khadim is an ancient site in south-western Sinai where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> mined turquoise from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> onwards. Its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>, 'Lady of Turquoise', was enlarged over many centuries. Inscriptions left there by Semitic-speaking workers, the Proto-Sinaitic inscriptions, are among the earliest alphabetic writing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serabit-el-khadim.jpg" alt="Serabit el-Khadim" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Einsamer Schütze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serabit_el-Chadim_019.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
